WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.859
&gt;&gt;Well, thank you very much, Dean Saloner.

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:08.390
As Garth said, I was with the advisory council several years

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.500
ago and got to know Garth very well.

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:14.240
And I can certainly attest to a great selection that was

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:18.120
made and Garth being the dean of our school.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.509
Well, good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:24.519
It's a pleasure to be back here at my alma mater and the

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:25.579
GSB.

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:30.099
In fact, I was a member of the first graduating class, back

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:35.419
in 1967, when this building first opened as the brand new

00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:38.079
Stanford Business School.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:38.300
&gt;&gt;

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.100
[laughter]

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.269
And it really makes you feel old as you come back and see

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:46.939
that the new school is soon to be opened.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.449
It is also ironic that early next year, as Garth mentioned,

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:54.469
just a few months from now, that I will retire from Wells

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:59.219
Fargo, 42 years after I first joined General Mills, having

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:02.049
just graduated from the GSB.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:07.530
And again, as Garth mentioned ? and I hope that you all,

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:12.219
when you are contemplating your next career move, would stay

00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:14.500
and be more flexible than I was.

00:01:14.519 --> 00:01:18.120
Because it is true that, when I was thinking of what to do

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.609
upon graduation, I really did not know for sure what I

00:01:21.629 --> 00:01:25.340
wanted to do, but I was very certain of what I did not want

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:26.359
to do.

00:01:26.359 --> 00:01:30.109
I was certain that I did not want to ever work for a bank or

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.039
work in New York City.

00:01:30.939 --> 00:01:31.569
&gt;&gt;

00:01:31.589 --> 00:01:32.589
[laughter]

00:01:31.589 --> 00:01:36.920
And a mere eight years later, I became an executive of

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:39.439
Citibank at 53rd and Park Avenue.

00:01:39.459 --> 00:01:41.500
So keep an open mind.

00:01:41.519 --> 00:01:42.500
&gt;&gt;

00:01:42.519 --> 00:01:43.519
[laughter]

00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:46.900
Since I have been around this business world a long time and

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.500
about ready to "hang it up," as we used to say on the

00:01:50.519 --> 00:01:53.269
athletic field, what I thought I would do this afternoon is

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.450
share some thoughts with you entitled, "What have I learned

00:01:56.469 --> 00:01:58.099
since business school?"

00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:01.859
I hope this does not cause you to quit school and ask for a

00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:03.709
tuition refund, however.

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:04.709
&gt;&gt;

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:05.729
[laughter]

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:08.529
As you will soon hear, I am quite passionate about certain

00:02:08.549 --> 00:02:11.859
ideas that have worked for me over my business career and

00:02:11.879 --> 00:02:15.539
have not usually been an important element of the curriculum

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.329
at leading graduate schools of business.

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:21.559
I began experimenting with these ideas shortly after my

00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:25.909
first general management job in 1969 at General Mills, but I

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:29.889
did not put all of these ideas into an actual marketplace

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:36.829
test until I joined Norwest in Minneapolis back in 1986 as

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:38.869
vice-chairman, head of the banking group there.

00:02:38.889 --> 00:02:47.069
Let me briefly summarize our results from 1986 to now.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.589
Our assets have grown from 20 billion to 1.3 trillion, our

00:02:51.609 --> 00:02:56.359
net income from a hundred million to an estimate of $12

00:02:56.379 --> 00:03:03.039
billion this year that analysts are forecasting, our market

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:09.659
cap from 800 million to 135 billion, a compound rate of 25

00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:10.679
percent.

00:03:10.679 --> 00:03:16.449
We had 15,000 team members back then; we have 282,000 today.

00:03:16.469 --> 00:03:21.059
We had 700 stores; today, we have 10,000.

00:03:21.079 --> 00:03:24.979
We have also today ranked as the 14th most admired company

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.999
in the Fortune Magazine's Annual Survey and Bearn's magazine

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:31.019
in the United States.

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:36.249
And Bearn's magazine ranks as one of the top 25 most-

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:37.709
respected companies in the world.

00:03:37.729 --> 00:03:42.809
Now, I mention these things only to demonstrate what I think

00:03:42.829 --> 00:03:46.079
is the power of some of the things that I have learned since

00:03:45.369 --> 00:03:46.519
business school.

00:03:46.539 --> 00:03:51.369
And these quantitative and qualitative measurements at least

00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:55.689
give an indication that there has been some value to those

00:03:55.709 --> 00:03:58.929
ideas.

00:03:58.949 --> 00:04:04.449
I would also ? I also want to mention that I?ve had the

00:04:04.469 --> 00:04:07.939
privilege of meeting the faculty of this great school on

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:11.399
many occasions and in many different ways where I have

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:17.329
spoken about what I thought were important changes that

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:21.439
needed to be made in the curriculum of all business schools.

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:25.400
And I'm pleased to see that the changes that have been made

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.420
by the GSB in the last five or ten years were very

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.199
consistent with some of the thoughts and ideas I'll convey

00:04:31.219 --> 00:04:33.439
to you today.

00:04:33.459 --> 00:04:37.340
What I learned at the GSB and in my engineering courses, in

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.980
terms of quantitative methods and finance, accounting,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.209
strategy, and marketing, were obviously of great value and

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:46.740
were very important, and I believe are very important to

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:48.009
business success.

00:04:48.029 --> 00:04:51.350
But on the margin, they were not even in the same ballpark

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.740
and important as the concepts of leadership, people as a

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:57.509
competitive advantage, and what I would describe as "

00:04:57.529 --> 00:04:59.530
management by culture."

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.800
But I am getting a little ahead of myself.

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:08.340
And I want to mention that I think one of the values of the

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.280
engineering background that I had, even though ? you should

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:17.180
all feel good that, those of you who cross bridges at least,

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:21.750
or work in tall skyscrapers ? that I was not a real engineer

00:05:21.769 --> 00:05:22.769
as such.

00:05:22.769 --> 00:05:27.410
I was an industrial engineer at Stanford, and industrial

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:31.250
engineering really was a quantitative application of

00:05:31.269 --> 00:05:32.470
business principles.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.240
In fact, many of the professors were in the both the

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.430
business school and the industrial engineering department.

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:41.269
And it was things like statistical analysis, quantitative

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:43.480
business methods, operations research, production

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.120
efficiency, computer science, and so on.

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:50.120
We all did take the same basic engineering courses of math,

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:52.629
physics, and so forth.

00:05:52.649 --> 00:05:55.730
But while my liberal arts classmates were playing volleyball

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.899
in the afternoon, we engineers were in the lab doing work.

00:05:59.919 --> 00:06:03.850
We were not on the baseball field, at least in my case.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.620
And, although I never practiced as an engineer, I do think

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:09.319
that the engineering background did significantly influence

00:06:09.339 --> 00:06:12.860
the process that I use in making decisions.

00:06:12.879 --> 00:06:16.240
And that process does appear to be different from that of

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:19.560
some other large companies' CEOs.

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:22.540
My engineering background influenced my extensive of use of

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.560
deductive reasoning, the importance of analyzing data

00:06:25.579 --> 00:06:29.370
intelligently, the ability to ask relevant questions to

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:35.300
challenge existing assumptions, and do sensitivity analysis

00:06:35.319 --> 00:06:39.410
on the various alternatives that could be modeled on what

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:40.889
decision to make.

00:06:40.909 --> 00:06:44.310
In short, to thoroughly understand the quantitative elements

00:06:44.329 --> 00:06:47.720
and the risk vs. reward aspects of the ultimate decision.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.629
But I also learned a lot outside the classroom, especially

00:06:51.649 --> 00:06:54.079
on the athletic field and through actual business

00:06:54.099 --> 00:06:58.089
experience, by making a lot of mistakes, but hopefully never

00:06:58.109 --> 00:07:01.740
making the same mistake twice, ie. learning from one's

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:03.370
mistakes.

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:06.660
The most important things I have learned in my 42 years in

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:10.649
business and 34 years in financial services is that, success

00:07:10.669 --> 00:07:13.360
in the business world ? especially in the world of financial

00:07:13.379 --> 00:07:17.589
services or other service companies ? is less about brains

00:07:17.609 --> 00:07:21.370
at the 99 percent level, and more about people development,

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:26.800
motivation, coaching, leadership, teamwork, integrity,

00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:31.829
culture, community involvement, vision, values, and a broad

00:07:31.849 --> 00:07:34.839
understanding of a variety of business disciplines,

00:07:34.859 --> 00:07:37.899
especially interpersonal relations.

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:41.040
Intelligence above the 99 percent level might even be

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.529
inversely correlated with some of these attributes.

00:07:43.549 --> 00:07:49.149
GMAT scores just don't measure these skills.

00:07:49.169 --> 00:07:51.850
In today's business world, we need broad generals to

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.469
successfully run large and complex companies who desperately

00:07:55.489 --> 00:07:57.100
need leaders.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.730
This is where sports comes in, at least for me.

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:03.069
From the age of 4 through 21, I played some form of

00:08:03.089 --> 00:08:05.990
competitive athletics for two to three hours almost every

00:08:06.009 --> 00:08:07.079
day.

00:08:07.099 --> 00:08:10.170
Sports helped me to understand human nature, teamwork, and

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:12.259
how emotions impact performance.

00:08:12.279 --> 00:08:14.819
Sports helped me to appreciate the value of personal

00:08:14.839 --> 00:08:18.129
discipline, physical conditioning, and the use of time.

00:08:18.149 --> 00:08:20.610
But, perhaps most importantly, athletics introduced me to

00:08:20.629 --> 00:08:23.659
the power of interpersonal dynamics.

00:08:23.679 --> 00:08:27.500
I experience, as I know many of you have, that teamwork and

00:08:27.519 --> 00:08:31.290
team spirit causes a group of individuals to perform well

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:35.029
above the level of their individual abilities.

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:38.009
In basketball, for instance, it's not the five best players

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:42.620
who become champions, it is the best five players who do so.

00:08:42.639 --> 00:08:45.139
So what does it take to be successful in business?

00:08:45.139 --> 00:08:48.639
Well, if I had to do it all over again, perhaps the MBA I

00:08:48.519 --> 00:08:51.399
received is not the best degree to lead a company to

00:08:51.419 --> 00:08:53.320
success, after all.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:57.740
Perhaps you should seek four very different degrees.

00:08:57.759 --> 00:09:00.539
First, get a degree in believing.

00:09:00.559 --> 00:09:05.960
Believe in the enormous talent of people, people at all

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.409
levels of your organization.

00:09:08.429 --> 00:09:11.720
To receive a degree in believing, your dissertation must

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.730
answer this question.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:16.649
"Why do many people enter business being bright, well-

00:09:16.669 --> 00:09:20.439
educated, energetic, wanting to make a difference, and in a

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:24.460
few years, feel bored, ignored, unfulfilled, and

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.000
unimportant? Why is that??

00:09:28.019 --> 00:09:31.830
Today's MBAs need a second degree: in affirming.

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:35.590
They must affirm their belief that almost everyone in any

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.710
organization, profit or not-for-profit, wants to contribute,

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:41.819
wants to do a good job.

00:09:41.839 --> 00:09:44.970
Receiving this degree requires that we treat everyone in our

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:49.220
organization with respect as equals, as talented people, as

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.279
important members of our team, working together toward a

00:09:52.299 --> 00:09:56.319
common goal, regardless of rank, education level, or

00:09:56.339 --> 00:09:58.169
compensation.

00:09:58.189 --> 00:10:01.870
Third, I would recommend a master's degree in "M and F.?

00:10:01.889 --> 00:10:06.439
That stands for "Motivation and Fun.?

00:10:06.459 --> 00:10:08.069
Yes, you heard correctly: Fun.

00:10:08.089 --> 00:10:12.189
I know it may be surprising to you, but even bankers should

00:10:12.209 --> 00:10:13.209
have fun.

00:10:13.209 --> 00:10:14.189
&gt;&gt;

00:10:14.209 --> 00:10:15.209
[laughter]

00:10:15.199 --> 00:10:17.949
I define "fun" as ?Working with people to help them get

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:21.689
excited about what they're doing, help create an enjoyable

00:10:21.709 --> 00:10:26.580
work environment, have fun, and then recognize them publicly

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:31.179
with great fanfare for a job well-done.?

00:10:31.199 --> 00:10:34.259
When people aren't having fun, when they're not recognized

00:10:34.279 --> 00:10:37.399
for outstanding performance, when no one says thanks, they

00:10:37.419 --> 00:10:41.200
do become disengaged and feel unimportant.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:45.309
Recognition, I believe is, on the margin, more important

00:10:45.329 --> 00:10:48.860
than salaries, benefits, and bonuses, because most all

00:10:48.879 --> 00:10:50.559
companies pay competitively.

00:10:50.579 --> 00:10:55.049
It is corporate America's most underutilized, motivational

00:10:55.069 --> 00:10:56.120
tool.

00:10:56.139 --> 00:10:59.860
Try thanking someone too much for a job well-done.

00:10:59.879 --> 00:11:01.129
You can't do it.

00:11:00.799 --> 00:11:04.529
So you need a master's degree in believing, affirming,

00:11:04.549 --> 00:11:06.009
motivation, and fun.

00:11:06.029 --> 00:11:09.220
And then, you need a doctorate, a doctorate in leadership,

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:13.059
the most advanced and important degree possible.

00:11:13.079 --> 00:11:14.580
What is leadership?

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:18.330
There are probably as many definitions as there are leaders.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:20.600
I'm sure many of you have your own.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.949
I define leadership as the "Act of establishing and

00:11:23.969 --> 00:11:27.809
communicating a vision of the future, and the art of

00:11:27.829 --> 00:11:32.569
motivating others to align with and embrace that vision."

00:11:32.589 --> 00:11:37.330
Leaders enable people to do such great things that they

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:40.259
believe that they can continue doing them.

00:11:40.279 --> 00:11:43.029
Leaders take people from where they are to where they've

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:44.159
never been.

00:11:44.179 --> 00:11:47.759
That's why leadership is the critical skill for sustainable

00:11:47.779 --> 00:11:49.830
business success.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:53.159
But great leaders give power to their teams; they do not

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:54.569
monopolize it.

00:11:54.589 --> 00:11:57.399
You cannot share a vision unless you share the power to

00:11:57.419 --> 00:11:58.419
achieve it.

00:11:58.419 --> 00:12:02.179
Leaders don't point fingers, they point direction.

00:12:02.199 --> 00:12:04.029
They show the way by personal example.

00:12:04.049 --> 00:12:08.049
And I'm sure everyone here today is still familiar with

00:12:08.069 --> 00:12:12.559
Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs, which I did learn in both my

00:12:12.579 --> 00:12:14.879
engineering and MBA classes.

00:12:14.899 --> 00:12:16.899
It arranges individual needs in a pyramid.

00:12:16.919 --> 00:12:19.919
And, as you well know, safety is at the bottom moving up

00:12:19.919 --> 00:12:22.669
through food, shelter, and at the top, a feeling of self-

00:12:21.919 --> 00:12:22.919
worth.

00:12:22.919 --> 00:12:26.169
In the same way, I believe there's a pyramid of tools to

00:12:26.039 --> 00:12:28.939
lead any business to greatness.

00:12:28.959 --> 00:12:31.659
Importantly, but at the bottom, there are the fundamentals

00:12:31.679 --> 00:12:34.569
of the business enterprise: the accounting, the computer

00:12:34.589 --> 00:12:36.899
literacy, quantitative skills.

00:12:36.919 --> 00:12:41.029
You move up the pyramid to finance, manufacturing skills,

00:12:41.049 --> 00:12:45.090
sales marketing, and then to the top, empowering people,

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:49.419
motivation, recognition, communication skills, and at the

00:12:49.439 --> 00:12:52.079
apex, leadership.

00:12:52.099 --> 00:12:55.240
The need for speed and adaptability in today's world leaves

00:12:55.259 --> 00:12:59.179
zero room, in my opinion, for dictatorship, centralized

00:12:59.199 --> 00:13:02.379
command and control, and in the worst sense of the word,

00:13:02.399 --> 00:13:03.669
hierarchy.

00:13:03.689 --> 00:13:06.439
Hierarchy assumes the higher up you are, the better you are

00:13:06.439 --> 00:13:07.439
at making decisions.

00:13:07.439 --> 00:13:11.980
Here's a secret: Almost every person at Wells Fargo knows

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.069
more about their specific work than I do: tellers,

00:13:15.089 --> 00:13:17.439
assistance people, commercial bankers, store managers, you

00:13:17.459 --> 00:13:18.459
name it.

00:13:18.459 --> 00:13:21.240
They all know more about the needs of their customers.

00:13:21.259 --> 00:13:24.419
Because they know more, there's no need to waste time coming

00:13:24.439 --> 00:13:26.029
to me for a decision.

00:13:26.049 --> 00:13:29.019
Make the decision yourself, and let me know what you've

00:13:29.039 --> 00:13:30.039
decided.

00:13:30.039 --> 00:13:33.289
If you think I can help you to make better decisions, I'm

00:13:33.179 --> 00:13:35.350
available to do so.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.419
I remember reading books in my MBA class that said, The

00:13:38.439 --> 00:13:41.210
maximum number of people who should report to someone else

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:44.759
to have what was called ?effective control,? was six to

00:13:44.779 --> 00:13:46.909
eight, but never more than ten.

00:13:46.929 --> 00:13:47.929
Baloney!

00:13:47.929 --> 00:13:53.169
It should be 15 to 20 at least, maybe even more.

00:13:53.189 --> 00:13:55.439
The more people who report to you, the better.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:58.049
It forces you to delegate, give responsibility, and not

00:13:58.069 --> 00:13:59.069
interfere.

00:13:59.069 --> 00:14:04.129
It allows others a satisfaction of running their own show

00:14:04.149 --> 00:14:07.759
and being responsible for their own results.

00:14:07.779 --> 00:14:11.439
It also reduces layers between you and the customer.

00:14:11.459 --> 00:14:14.209
The truth is, I didn't run Wells Fargo.

00:14:14.229 --> 00:14:17.340
We have nearly one hundred businesses, each with their own

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:19.919
fully-loaded profit and loss.

00:14:19.939 --> 00:14:22.079
We are very decentralized.

00:14:22.099 --> 00:14:24.639
The business heads are totally responsible for their

00:14:24.659 --> 00:14:25.730
businesses.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:29.549
They report to six group heads, who are the CEOs for their

00:14:29.569 --> 00:14:32.699
group of businesses, and they should act more like coaches

00:14:32.719 --> 00:14:34.490
than bosses.

00:14:34.509 --> 00:14:37.970
My job was to select the best people to run those businesses

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:41.569
and those groups, let them do it, coach them so they learn

00:14:41.589 --> 00:14:44.730
even faster, and assure we have a strong internal check and

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:47.919
balance audit process that verifies that they are adhering

00:14:47.939 --> 00:14:51.980
to the principles and the policies that we've agreed upon.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:55.009
People at the top should, above all, be leaders; quite

00:14:55.029 --> 00:14:57.439
often, they act like managers.

00:14:57.459 --> 00:15:00.419
Managers administer; leaders innovate.

00:15:00.439 --> 00:15:04.419
Managers rely on systems; leaders rely on people.

00:15:04.439 --> 00:15:08.350
Managers need control; leaders rely on trust.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:12.049
Managers work on getting things right; leaders work on the

00:15:12.069 --> 00:15:13.959
right things.

00:15:13.979 --> 00:15:16.899
At Wells Fargo, we believe personal leadership is the key to

00:15:16.919 --> 00:15:17.919
success.

00:15:17.919 --> 00:15:20.539
We believe the answer to every problem, issue, or

00:15:20.559 --> 00:15:24.250
opportunity in our company is already known by some person

00:15:24.269 --> 00:15:25.769
or team in the company.

00:15:25.789 --> 00:15:29.289
The leader only has to find that person, listen, and help

00:15:29.309 --> 00:15:30.779
effect the change.

00:15:30.799 --> 00:15:34.090
By the way, the people with the best answers are not always

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.730
the people with the most stripes.

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:39.799
True leaders do not demand loyalty, they create it.

00:15:39.819 --> 00:15:42.689
They use conflict among diverse points of view to enable the

00:15:42.709 --> 00:15:44.470
team to reach new insights.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.529
They exert influence by reinforcing values.

00:15:47.549 --> 00:15:49.620
At Wells Fargo, we manage these hundred different

00:15:49.639 --> 00:15:52.449
businesses, spanning a wide geography across all of North

00:15:52.469 --> 00:15:55.959
America and internationally, by sharing a corporate culture

00:15:55.979 --> 00:16:00.199
that people believe in and align with, not by command and

00:16:00.219 --> 00:16:02.990
control or hierarchy.

00:16:03.009 --> 00:16:06.269
A good leader inspires a team to have confidence in him or

00:16:06.289 --> 00:16:10.059
her; a great leader inspires a team to have confidence in

00:16:10.079 --> 00:16:11.699
themselves.

00:16:11.719 --> 00:16:13.100
This may surprise you.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:18.329
At Wells Fargo, our most important constituent is not our

00:16:18.349 --> 00:16:20.639
customers; it's our people.

00:16:20.659 --> 00:16:23.360
Because our people have the greatest influence on our

00:16:23.379 --> 00:16:27.459
customers and how our customers perceive Wells Fargo.

00:16:27.479 --> 00:16:30.750
Our people influence the attitude of our customers more than

00:16:30.769 --> 00:16:33.289
anything else.

00:16:33.309 --> 00:16:37.100
Our people are key to customer satisfaction and increasing

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:38.679
market share.

00:16:38.699 --> 00:16:43.110
So, my job as CEO of the past 15 years was really quite

00:16:43.129 --> 00:16:44.129
simple:

00:16:44.129 --> 00:16:47.069
Cause the organization to focus on customers.

00:16:47.089 --> 00:16:50.149
Recognize that the key to consistent bottom line growth is

00:16:50.169 --> 00:16:52.169
actually the top line: revenue growth.

00:16:52.189 --> 00:16:57.279
Hire, retain, reward, and recognize the best people.

00:16:57.299 --> 00:16:59.629
Coach them to be effective leaders.

00:16:59.649 --> 00:17:01.639
Make sure they care.

00:17:01.659 --> 00:17:05.129
In selecting people, I don't care how much a person knows

00:17:05.149 --> 00:17:07.740
until I know how much they care.

00:17:07.759 --> 00:17:11.029
Give people the responsibility, an accounted way to run

00:17:11.049 --> 00:17:12.839
their own business.

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:15.639
Encourage them to be active in their communities, to be

00:17:15.659 --> 00:17:17.569
leaders in their communities.

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:20.899
Create, describe, and communicate the corporate culture, its

00:17:20.919 --> 00:17:22.250
vision and values.

00:17:22.269 --> 00:17:24.250
Make sure everyone is having some fun.

00:17:24.269 --> 00:17:29.299
Then, just get out of their way and cheer, as they achieve

00:17:29.319 --> 00:17:33.109
consistent, record-breaking, industry-leading results.

00:17:33.129 --> 00:17:36.129
In short, what I learned on the athletic fields and from 40-

00:17:36.029 --> 00:17:39.619
plus years in the business world is that, success is all

00:17:39.639 --> 00:17:43.500
about people, especially in financial services or other

00:17:43.519 --> 00:17:45.009
service businesses.

00:17:45.029 --> 00:17:47.549
At Wells Fargo, we call it, "People as a competitive

00:17:47.569 --> 00:17:48.769
advantage.?

00:17:48.789 --> 00:17:52.500
Few, if any, of other large financial institutions really

00:17:52.519 --> 00:17:54.450
walk that talk.

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:57.220
Our emphasis on people is the foundation of our company's

00:17:57.129 --> 00:17:59.879
culture, our growth, and our success.

00:17:59.899 --> 00:18:03.579
Because we are so decentralized, and rely for results on

00:18:03.599 --> 00:18:05.700
individual business leaders ? which number in the thousands

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:09.259
by the way ? we manage the overall company by culture,

00:18:09.279 --> 00:18:11.849
shared vision, and values, and coaching.

00:18:11.869 --> 00:18:15.649
Incidentally, I don't remember these concepts ever being

00:18:15.669 --> 00:18:18.299
presented, let alone taught, when I was at the Stanford

00:18:18.319 --> 00:18:19.879
Business School.

00:18:19.899 --> 00:18:23.319
So, what I would now like to do is, give you some examples

00:18:23.339 --> 00:18:26.470
of how culture influences our behavior and decision-making

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:27.649
process.

00:18:27.669 --> 00:18:32.099
I believe these examples apply to all businesses, but again,

00:18:32.119 --> 00:18:35.829
in particular, those that have a direct service relationship

00:18:35.849 --> 00:18:39.430
with their customers.

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:42.569
Take acquisition strategy, for example.

00:18:42.589 --> 00:18:46.609
At Wells Fargo, we will only do those acquisitions where the

00:18:46.629 --> 00:18:49.950
company that we are considering acquiring has a culture that

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.129
is similar to Wells Fargo's.

00:18:52.149 --> 00:18:57.950
Our culture is described in this "Vision and Values" booklet

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:01.009
that is updated about every two years and was first

00:19:01.029 --> 00:19:03.299
published about twenty years ago.

00:19:03.319 --> 00:19:07.039
It describes in great detail our vision, values, culture,

00:19:07.059 --> 00:19:08.680
and operating philosophy.

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:12.450
If any one of you is interested in a copy of this, I have a

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:17.069
few of them here.

00:19:17.089 --> 00:19:22.089
So, early on in the discussion stage of a possible

00:19:22.109 --> 00:19:26.700
acquisition, we will give the principals this booklet and

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.659
ask them to compare their culture to ours.

00:19:29.679 --> 00:19:32.679
We ask them to estimate how well such a vision and values

00:19:32.409 --> 00:19:33.639
will be accepted.

00:19:33.659 --> 00:19:36.549
If the cultures are considered to be incompatible, we would

00:19:36.569 --> 00:19:39.180
terminate further discussions.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:42.200
A prime example of the role of culture in our acquisition

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:45.269
decision-making process was when other large banks were

00:19:45.289 --> 00:19:49.889
buying investment banks in the late 1990's; we did not.

00:19:49.909 --> 00:19:52.960
We felt that the investment banking culture was incompatible

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:54.190
with ours.

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:56.920
We are a relationship-focused company; investment banks are

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:58.559
often transactionally-focused.

00:19:58.579 --> 00:20:01.409
We are a team-focused company with team incentives.

00:20:01.429 --> 00:20:04.009
Investment banks are often made up of independent-minded,

00:20:04.029 --> 00:20:07.200
highly-paid stars, who are individually-incented.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:10.079
Some investment banks often cross the line, in our belief,

00:20:10.099 --> 00:20:13.460
in terms of ethical behavior, and often take excessive risk

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:15.240
relative to rewards.

00:20:15.259 --> 00:20:18.430
We predicted that six out of ten of these investment bank

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:21.180
acquisitions by commercial banks would not work.

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:22.500
We were wrong.

00:20:22.519 --> 00:20:24.269
Nine out of ten didn't work.

00:20:23.909 --> 00:20:24.109
&gt;&gt;

00:20:24.109 --> 00:20:25.109
[laughter]

00:20:24.109 --> 00:20:30.149
In fact, that's how we missed the mess other large banks and

00:20:30.169 --> 00:20:32.049
investment banks are in right now.

00:20:32.069 --> 00:20:34.680
?Management by culture? told us that some of the actions

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:38.980
taken by others were morally and ethically wrong.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:42.819
Other actions taken just did not make any risk / reward

00:20:42.839 --> 00:20:44.250
sense to us.

00:20:44.269 --> 00:20:47.089
Management by culture saved us.

00:20:47.109 --> 00:20:49.039
But, things change.

00:20:49.059 --> 00:20:51.309
Because of these culture and ethical lapses, the investment

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:53.599
banking industry has dramatically changed.

00:20:53.619 --> 00:20:55.869
With the recent demise of Merrill Lynch, Lehman Brothers,

00:20:55.759 --> 00:21:01.119
Bear Stearns and many smaller firms that actually left us in

00:21:01.139 --> 00:21:04.259
the 1990's, there are only two broad, product line,

00:21:04.279 --> 00:21:06.720
investment banks left, namely Goldman Sachs and Morgan

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:10.159
Stanley, and they are actually bank holding companies today.

00:21:10.179 --> 00:21:12.920
We believe that this radical industry realignment has

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.369
permanently changed the competitive environment and business

00:21:16.389 --> 00:21:19.379
practices, such that Wells Fargo can now be in the

00:21:19.399 --> 00:21:22.759
investment banking business without compromising our culture

00:21:22.779 --> 00:21:23.899
and values.

00:21:23.919 --> 00:21:28.039
In combination with Wachovia, in just the past year, and

00:21:28.059 --> 00:21:31.759
because of the investment banking expertise that Wachovia

00:21:31.779 --> 00:21:35.349
had, we have gone from being a very small player to likely

00:21:35.369 --> 00:21:38.930
ranking in the top ten of US investment banks this year, and

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:42.450
we think we?ll be in the top five within the next few years.

00:21:42.309 --> 00:21:45.500
Our company's culture and belief in ?people as a competitive

00:21:45.519 --> 00:21:48.559
advantage? also causes us to handle layoffs and staff

00:21:48.579 --> 00:21:50.529
reductions different from others.

00:21:50.549 --> 00:21:53.409
Now, we know it is often necessary, because of acquisitions

00:21:53.429 --> 00:21:57.109
or other downsizing, that positions must be eliminated, but

00:21:57.129 --> 00:22:00.470
eliminating positions does not necessarily mean that good,

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:04.519
loyal, experienced, and talented people need to be fired.

00:22:04.539 --> 00:22:07.430
When our competitors do acquisitions, they can hardly wait

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:10.450
to tell The Street how many people will be fired and what

00:22:10.329 --> 00:22:12.339
the cost savings will be.

00:22:12.359 --> 00:22:15.740
We, in contrast, don't typically do acquisitions for cost

00:22:15.759 --> 00:22:18.029
savings but rather for revenue growth.

00:22:18.049 --> 00:22:21.409
Cost savings are usually a one-time profit gain.

00:22:21.429 --> 00:22:24.680
Revenue growth is the gift that keeps on giving.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:28.139
We do eliminate positions, but we attempt to find other jobs

00:22:28.159 --> 00:22:30.119
for the people being displaced.

00:22:30.139 --> 00:22:33.309
To find other jobs for the people being displaced, given the

00:22:33.329 --> 00:22:35.579
turnover that exists in our company, there are nearly

00:22:35.399 --> 00:22:38.710
thousands of positions available each year that can be

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:42.930
filled by people displaced by acquisitions and downsizing.

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:45.889
We call it "Retain and Retrain."

00:22:45.909 --> 00:22:48.409
Since we believe in people as a competitive advantage, we

00:22:48.259 --> 00:22:51.789
want to keep all the good, experienced people that we can on

00:22:51.809 --> 00:22:53.210
our team.

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:55.730
Another area we differ from other banks was our early

00:22:55.609 --> 00:22:58.759
commitment to, and continued belief that, we are a financial

00:22:58.779 --> 00:23:01.940
services company much more than a bank.

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:05.119
For over two decades now, less than 50 percent of our

00:23:05.139 --> 00:23:07.809
revenue comes from traditional banking.

00:23:07.829 --> 00:23:11.149
Traditional banking is about a $500 billion industry.

00:23:11.169 --> 00:23:14.190
The total financial services industry, which, in addition to

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:16.710
banking, includes insurance, investment banking, asset

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:19.680
management, mortgage, consumer finance, leasing, and other

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:24.829
products, is a $2.7 trillion in size, over five times larger

00:23:24.849 --> 00:23:29.759
than banking.

00:23:29.779 --> 00:23:33.839
The leading bank in a given state may have 20 percent to 30

00:23:33.859 --> 00:23:37.099
percent banking share in that state.

00:23:37.119 --> 00:23:40.119
It is hard to grow when you have such a large share.

00:23:39.289 --> 00:23:42.639
However, this banking share leader would usually have less

00:23:42.659 --> 00:23:46.019
than a five percent financial services market share in that

00:23:46.039 --> 00:23:47.039
state.

00:23:47.039 --> 00:23:50.289
That means there's plenty of room for growth if you are a

00:23:50.089 --> 00:23:51.089
financial services company.

00:23:51.089 --> 00:23:54.769
While others follow the advice of Wall Street, consulting

00:23:54.789 --> 00:23:57.859
firms, and conventional business school teaching, being

00:23:57.879 --> 00:24:02.859
strategically focused ie. a narrow product line, and some

00:24:02.879 --> 00:24:06.509
were basically monolines, Wells Fargo has maintained a very

00:24:06.529 --> 00:24:10.109
broad product line, delivering about every financial product

00:24:10.129 --> 00:24:13.609
and service purchased by mass market and high-net-worth

00:24:13.629 --> 00:24:17.349
consumers, as well as large, medium, and small businesses.

00:24:17.369 --> 00:24:20.619
A lot of people, including some of my Stanford Business

00:24:20.639 --> 00:24:23.389
School professors, think you shouldn't try to be all things

00:24:23.389 --> 00:24:24.389
to all people.

00:24:24.389 --> 00:24:28.700
Admittedly, it is hard to do, but if you can figure out how

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:31.490
to effectively deliver, through cross-selling, all the

00:24:31.509 --> 00:24:35.649
financial products needed by all the potential customers in

00:24:35.669 --> 00:24:39.319
your marketplace, there are enormous competitive advantages,

00:24:39.339 --> 00:24:43.519
diversity of income streams, economics of scale and skill,

00:24:43.539 --> 00:24:46.250
and compelling profitability.

00:24:46.269 --> 00:24:50.319
Through my positions on Board of Directors and the number of

00:24:50.339 --> 00:24:54.649
customer relationships that we have, I have helped to nudge

00:24:54.669 --> 00:25:00.319
other companies to consider expanding their strategy and

00:25:00.339 --> 00:25:03.579
selling more products to their existing customers and more

00:25:03.599 --> 00:25:06.220
services, and it has also worked for them.

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:11.049
It has always fascinated me that a multi-product, financial

00:25:11.069 --> 00:25:14.240
company like Wells Fargo was considered by some as almost a

00:25:14.259 --> 00:25:17.669
conglomerate: too broad, not focused.

00:25:17.689 --> 00:25:21.109
So I?d ask these people, "Is Wal-Mart a conglomerate?"

00:25:21.129 --> 00:25:24.349
?No,? they would say.

00:25:24.369 --> 00:25:25.369
?Are they focused??

00:25:25.169 --> 00:25:27.649
?Absolutely. They are focused.?

00:25:27.669 --> 00:25:33.509
?Well, what does Wal-Mart not sell??

00:25:33.529 --> 00:25:39.409
I try to get people to understand that Wells Fargo is, first

00:25:39.429 --> 00:25:40.919
and foremost, a distributor.

00:25:40.939 --> 00:25:43.789
It's a distributor of commodity financial products.

00:25:43.809 --> 00:25:46.569
Most all financial products are similar.

00:25:46.589 --> 00:25:49.929
Thus, we have much more in common with other distributors of

00:25:49.949 --> 00:25:52.779
commodity products, like Wal-Mart and Lowe's, than we did

00:25:52.799 --> 00:25:55.069
with Goldman Sachs or Countrywide.

00:25:55.089 --> 00:25:58.609
Just like Wal-Mart and Lowe's, our uniqueness is the way we

00:25:58.629 --> 00:26:03.589
distribute those products, not the products themselves.

00:26:03.609 --> 00:26:06.480
Consistent, organic, revenue growth through cross-selling is

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:09.519
probably Wells Fargo's most distinctive skill.

00:26:09.539 --> 00:26:13.159
Our average retail household has 5.9 products and over one

00:26:13.179 --> 00:26:15.079
in four has over eight products.

00:26:15.099 --> 00:26:18.839
These are, by far, the highest cross-sell ratios in the

00:26:18.859 --> 00:26:21.599
industry and about twice the industry average.

00:26:21.619 --> 00:26:25.190
Our average wholesale customer has 6.4 products with us.

00:26:25.210 --> 00:26:27.700
Our middle-market, commercial banking, division averages

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:31.950
close to ten products per customer.

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:34.720
Again, about double the industry average.

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:37.720
Organic, double-digit, revenue growth is essential for

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:40.619
consistent, double-digit, profit growth.

00:26:40.639 --> 00:26:43.559
We have grown our revenue at a double-digit, compound rate

00:26:43.579 --> 00:26:47.659
for 20 years, and therefore, we have had the profitability

00:26:47.679 --> 00:26:52.109
and market share growth that I?ve already mentioned to you.

00:26:52.129 --> 00:26:56.869
Since many of monolines have crashed and burned, or been

00:26:56.889 --> 00:27:01.200
acquired, more companies now seem to be interested in cross-

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:03.419
selling and broadening their product line.

00:27:03.439 --> 00:27:06.490
We have a 20 year head start on these Johnny-come-latelies,

00:27:06.509 --> 00:27:08.700
and I'm glad we do.

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:12.919
Let me now mention the need for disciplined acquisitions.

00:27:12.939 --> 00:27:16.429
This is required in any company in any industry.

00:27:16.449 --> 00:27:19.659
I never understood the comment, "I know we paid a lot for

00:27:19.679 --> 00:27:24.569
this deal, but it is a strategic acquisition.?

00:27:24.589 --> 00:27:28.089
If you paid so much that you don't get a decent return, what

00:27:27.919 --> 00:27:30.250
could be strategic about it?

00:27:30.269 --> 00:27:34.259
A strategic deal should mean that it fits so well, you

00:27:34.279 --> 00:27:36.379
should make a pile of money.

00:27:36.399 --> 00:27:39.509
Let me ask you this about acquisition strategy.

00:27:39.529 --> 00:27:42.779
If you believe, as I do, that practically all financial

00:27:42.799 --> 00:27:45.609
segments ? and it?s true of many other industries ? are

00:27:45.629 --> 00:27:50.990
cyclical, when during the cycle should you acquire? ? at the

00:27:51.009 --> 00:27:55.529
top, or at the bottom?

00:27:55.549 --> 00:27:57.029
When do most people buy?

00:27:57.049 --> 00:28:01.329
I'm not going to ask you how many get that question right;

00:28:01.349 --> 00:28:04.589
I'll leave that to your classroom.

00:28:04.609 --> 00:28:07.909
Most people only want to buy great companies, and so do we;

00:28:07.929 --> 00:28:09.679
but, of course, they pay high premiums.

00:28:09.609 --> 00:28:14.309
Wells Fargo often buys fixer-uppers, companies who?ve had a

00:28:14.329 --> 00:28:16.909
temporary set back, but still have good people and good

00:28:16.929 --> 00:28:19.099
customers and a solid franchise.

00:28:19.119 --> 00:28:22.299
We pay a lower price, because there is more work to do,

00:28:22.319 --> 00:28:24.319
which will require more time and perhaps additional

00:28:24.259 --> 00:28:25.259
investment.

00:28:25.259 --> 00:28:28.720
But with our broad product line, our superior business

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:32.299
model, our strong, experienced management team, and our

00:28:32.319 --> 00:28:35.889
cross-selling prowess, we can usually, within three years,

00:28:35.909 --> 00:28:38.589
get the troubled company growing revenue at double digit

00:28:38.609 --> 00:28:40.950
rates, just like the rest of our company.

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:43.710
A good example of this strategy, that Garth mentioned, was

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:47.299
the acquisition of Wachovia Bank last fall for $12.7

00:28:47.319 --> 00:28:51.429
billion, a company whose market cap was over $90 billion a

00:28:51.449 --> 00:28:54.769
couple years before that, and has the number one banking

00:28:54.789 --> 00:28:58.399
market share in eight, fast-growing, southeastern states as

00:28:58.419 --> 00:29:00.399
well as other great markets.

00:29:00.419 --> 00:29:03.419
Yes, we have some problems to fix, but I believe this will

00:29:03.439 --> 00:29:07.049
turn out to be the best and most profitable acquisition in

00:29:07.069 --> 00:29:11.659
banking history and among the best in all of corporate

00:29:11.679 --> 00:29:12.819
history.

00:29:12.839 --> 00:29:16.429
We also often buy smaller companies rather than larger

00:29:16.449 --> 00:29:18.789
companies, because their culture is more similar to ours,

00:29:18.809 --> 00:29:22.250
the implementation risks are less, and so is the risk of

00:29:22.269 --> 00:29:23.349
being wrong.

00:29:23.369 --> 00:29:26.859
Most of our large bank competitors buy only large companies.

00:29:26.879 --> 00:29:31.189
When we do a deal, the financial projections must clear two

00:29:31.209 --> 00:29:36.129
hurdles: a minimum 15 percent internal rate of return and

00:29:36.149 --> 00:29:39.419
accreted to existing stockholders by no later than year

00:29:39.439 --> 00:29:40.480
three.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:42.029
Both are important.

00:29:42.049 --> 00:29:44.960
If you have a PE ratio higher than your acquisition target,

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:49.099
it is relatively easy to be accreted, but there is usually a

00:29:49.119 --> 00:29:52.179
reason for the PE difference: the target has probably been

00:29:52.199 --> 00:29:54.279
growing at a slower rate.

00:29:54.299 --> 00:29:57.419
If you give the sellers most of your PE difference, you will

00:29:57.439 --> 00:29:59.839
soon grow more slowly as well.

00:29:59.859 --> 00:30:01.730
Your PE will fall.

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:05.889
Requiring both an IRR and an accretive ____ rate, ensures

00:30:05.909 --> 00:30:08.909
you only do deals that will add value no matter how you

00:30:08.839 --> 00:30:12.460
elect to pay for them: stock, cash, or the combination of

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:13.480
both.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.279
Most companies don't do that.

00:30:15.299 --> 00:30:18.470
Some companies set their IRR hurdle rate too low, in my

00:30:18.490 --> 00:30:22.389
opinion, at their so-called ?cost of capital,? something

00:30:22.409 --> 00:30:25.679
they probably learned in business school as I did.

00:30:25.699 --> 00:30:28.319
Who wants to earn only their cost of capital?

00:30:28.339 --> 00:30:31.500
That's just sure step toward mediocrity.

00:30:31.519 --> 00:30:34.809
Others think any acquisition that is accretive is good,

00:30:34.829 --> 00:30:39.369
without considering the inherent PE differences.

00:30:39.389 --> 00:30:40.889
Let me ask you this question.

00:30:40.699 --> 00:30:43.569
Should you take actions based upon the advice of so-called

00:30:43.589 --> 00:30:47.220
industry visionaries, pundits, the media, and Wall Street

00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:48.240
experts?

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:52.299
When I first started running major bank businesses in the

00:30:52.319 --> 00:30:57.309
late 1970s, I was told by my boss that branches were dead

00:30:57.329 --> 00:31:00.450
and paper checks were history; we should start reducing both

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:01.470
immediately.

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:04.599
Well, there are more branches today than there have ever

00:31:04.619 --> 00:31:07.200
been, and they increase every year.

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:11.929
Only in the past couple years, 35 years later, are we seeing

00:31:11.949 --> 00:31:13.869
paper checks decline.

00:31:13.889 --> 00:31:16.889
In the 1980s, I was told that the mortgage business is a

00:31:16.269 --> 00:31:18.519
lousy business and we should get out of it.

00:31:18.379 --> 00:31:21.379
In the early 1990's I was told that banking margins were

00:31:21.039 --> 00:31:24.299
falling so rapidly, and non-banking competitors were growing

00:31:24.319 --> 00:31:27.509
market share so fast, that banking was all about cutting

00:31:27.529 --> 00:31:28.529
cost.

00:31:28.529 --> 00:31:31.000
The cost cutters would ultimately win.

00:31:31.019 --> 00:31:34.569
In the late 1990's, I was told that the Internet would make

00:31:34.589 --> 00:31:35.619
branches obsolete.

00:31:35.639 --> 00:31:39.069
Bill Gates said banks were dinosaurs.

00:31:39.089 --> 00:31:41.579
By the early 2000s, everything was reversed.

00:31:41.599 --> 00:31:45.189
It was now all about revenue growth, not cost cutting.

00:31:45.209 --> 00:31:47.959
I was also told branches were now so valuable, we should

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:49.799
open them by the thousands.

00:31:49.819 --> 00:31:52.449
As we know, those visionaries were consistently wrong.

00:31:52.469 --> 00:31:55.949
Ladies and gentlemen, it is important to listen to the

00:31:55.969 --> 00:31:58.470
futurists, the pundits, the media, and Wall Street, because

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:01.279
there is usually some rationale to their positions, but in

00:32:01.299 --> 00:32:04.259
my opinion, don't do anything until you see signs that

00:32:04.279 --> 00:32:07.009
customers are actually voting with their feet.

00:32:07.029 --> 00:32:10.009
Look for the earliest possible signs of radical change.

00:32:10.029 --> 00:32:15.099
If you don't see any, stay away from the bleeding edge.

00:32:15.119 --> 00:32:17.189
Customers, I said, use their feet.

00:32:17.209 --> 00:32:22.750
Don't look at eyeballs as a reason for market evaluations.

00:32:22.769 --> 00:32:25.669
Contrary to the industry, we've been opening new stores and

00:32:25.689 --> 00:32:28.549
acquiring stores through acquisitions at the same pace

00:32:28.569 --> 00:32:31.960
practically for two decades, and we are continuing to do so.

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:34.809
We never stopped or restarted, and we?ve always focused on

00:32:34.829 --> 00:32:37.000
revenue over cost cutting

00:32:37.019 --> 00:32:39.480
We also developed what is now considered the best,

00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:42.309
integrated Internet banking channel in the industry, and it

00:32:42.329 --> 00:32:44.679
serves all of our customer?s personal, business, and

00:32:44.699 --> 00:32:47.529
investment needs through one single sign-on, whether that

00:32:47.549 --> 00:32:49.869
person enters for their own personal accounts, their

00:32:49.889 --> 00:32:52.339
business accounts, or their wealth management accounts.

00:32:52.359 --> 00:32:55.109
Customers can navigate from one to the other easily and

00:32:55.129 --> 00:32:56.279
efficiently.

00:32:56.299 --> 00:32:59.059
Global Finance Magazine just rated our Internet offering as

00:32:59.079 --> 00:33:01.139
the best in the world.

00:33:01.159 --> 00:33:04.720
During the Internet glory days, unlike the pundits and

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:07.389
consulting companies and many of our competitors, we never

00:33:07.409 --> 00:33:10.039
saw the Internet as a competing channel.

00:33:10.059 --> 00:33:14.869
We saw it as complementing our other distribution channels.

00:33:14.889 --> 00:33:18.889
And the vast majority of all of our customers are actually

00:33:18.909 --> 00:33:20.389
multiple-channel users.

00:33:20.409 --> 00:33:23.079
They use our stores, the Internet, the ATMs, and the

00:33:23.099 --> 00:33:24.210
telephone.

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:28.210
We never saw, nor expected customers to suddenly stop using

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:31.659
their primary channel and immediately go exclusively to a

00:33:31.679 --> 00:33:32.779
new channel.

00:33:32.799 --> 00:33:35.710
We believed that if they did begin to shift, they would

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:39.949
shift gradually, and in such a moderate pace, that we could

00:33:39.969 --> 00:33:42.740
reconfigure our distribution network accordingly.

00:33:42.759 --> 00:33:45.429
Many of our competitors, during the Internet craze, closed

00:33:45.449 --> 00:33:50.069
their branches in anticipation of a major change in customer

00:33:50.089 --> 00:33:54.309
behavior, and then reversed all of that a few years later.

00:33:54.329 --> 00:33:57.319
Let me now mention Scale vs. Skill.

00:33:57.339 --> 00:34:02.079
How many times do you hear a CEO say, ?I need to get bigger

00:34:02.099 --> 00:34:03.740
to be better??

00:34:03.759 --> 00:34:05.119
I don't buy that.

00:34:05.139 --> 00:34:08.900
You get bigger by being better; you don't get better by

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:10.469
being bigger.

00:34:10.489 --> 00:34:13.139
Scale is certainly important in some business, but is often

00:34:13.159 --> 00:34:14.159
overrated.

00:34:14.159 --> 00:34:17.710
For example, in banking, I would say over a hundred billion

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:21.900
dollars in assets or so, skill is much more important than

00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:23.019
scale.

00:34:23.039 --> 00:34:26.329
Skill is finding the best way to do something in one place,

00:34:26.349 --> 00:34:29.619
say a store or with a customer, and then replicating that

00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:31.110
over your entire network.

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:34.179
That's the only advantage, to me, of being big.

00:34:34.199 --> 00:34:37.940
You get to take a good idea and multiply it thousands of

00:34:37.960 --> 00:34:41.099
times vs. maybe less than a hundred times for a smaller

00:34:41.119 --> 00:34:42.159
company.

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:45.639
Otherwise, size can be a deterrent to organic growth.

00:34:45.659 --> 00:34:46.659
Why?

00:34:46.659 --> 00:34:49.159
Because you often lose touch with your customers and team

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:49.500
members.

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:52.980
Bureaucracy sets in, hierarchy rules, you lose speed to

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:54.429
market and fast decision-making.

00:34:54.449 --> 00:34:56.980
How about risk management?

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:00.429
Well, you can't be considered the best financial services

00:35:00.449 --> 00:35:03.230
company in the industry unless you're the best at managing

00:35:03.250 --> 00:35:06.819
risk, risk of all forms: credit risk, interest rate risk,

00:35:06.839 --> 00:35:08.639
and operational risk.

00:35:08.659 --> 00:35:11.110
All segments of the financial services industry are

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:14.319
cyclical, and it's quite common that towards the end of the

00:35:14.339 --> 00:35:17.259
positive side of the cycle, asset values get elevated and

00:35:17.279 --> 00:35:20.779
risk spreads decline, to a level beyond ? and in some cases

00:35:20.799 --> 00:35:23.309
far beyond ? their fundamental levels.

00:35:23.329 --> 00:35:26.219
Only if you are disciplined, diversified, and have skilled

00:35:26.239 --> 00:35:29.739
and experienced managers and leaders that have reduced your

00:35:29.759 --> 00:35:33.539
exposure to such overvaluations, can you avoid the

00:35:33.559 --> 00:35:35.989
writedowns, as these assets are eventually valued

00:35:36.009 --> 00:35:38.130
significantly lower.

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:41.880
You only have to look at what happened in late 2007, when

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:44.650
the collapse of the subprime mortgage markets set off a re-

00:35:44.549 --> 00:35:47.579
evaluation of all asset classes, and led to a rather

00:35:47.599 --> 00:35:50.819
extended, credit crunch.

00:35:50.839 --> 00:35:53.199
We bankers just don't get it.

00:35:53.219 --> 00:35:56.309
Every decade or so, we have to relearn that covenants are

00:35:56.329 --> 00:35:59.150
important, that risks do go up and down, but never

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:02.019
disappear, that the Greater Fool Theory, now known as

00:36:02.039 --> 00:36:05.139
securitizations, collateral debt obligations, and the like,

00:36:05.159 --> 00:36:09.079
do not last long, and that sooner, but most often later,

00:36:09.099 --> 00:36:10.849
they will all come back to roost.

00:36:10.849 --> 00:36:13.799
Wells Fargo, by the way, did not participate in this

00:36:13.819 --> 00:36:14.819
charade.

00:36:14.819 --> 00:36:17.289
Our responsible-lending values, that are included in our

00:36:17.309 --> 00:36:20.289
vision and values booklet, saved us.

00:36:20.309 --> 00:36:23.599
We did not offer any no-doc option, negative-amortization

00:36:23.619 --> 00:36:25.480
loans, to subprime borrowers.

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:27.750
These exotic, subprime mortgage loans were not only

00:36:27.769 --> 00:36:30.429
economically unsound, they were not appropriate for many

00:36:30.449 --> 00:36:31.449
borrowers.

00:36:31.449 --> 00:36:36.279
We lost 4% market share in our mortgage business for three

00:36:36.299 --> 00:36:41.809
years between 2005 and 7, $160 billion in originations in

00:36:41.829 --> 00:36:43.840
2006 alone.

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:46.489
In hindsight, we were glad we did.

00:36:46.509 --> 00:36:49.650
For similar reasons, we also did not participate, to any

00:36:49.670 --> 00:36:53.449
significant degree, in the latest Wall Street fads, such as

00:36:53.469 --> 00:36:56.539
collateral debt obligations, structured investment vehicles

00:36:56.559 --> 00:37:01.250
to hold assets off balance sheets, hedge funds financing,

00:37:01.269 --> 00:37:04.539
off-balance-sheet conduits, low-covenant or no-covenant,

00:37:04.559 --> 00:37:07.750
large, highly-leveraged loans.

00:37:07.769 --> 00:37:10.519
We do not hold in our money market mutual funds, any

00:37:10.150 --> 00:37:12.630
collateralized debt obligations, and commercial paper

00:37:12.650 --> 00:37:15.679
obligations directly backed by subprime debt, and so on, and

00:37:15.699 --> 00:37:17.389
so on.

00:37:17.409 --> 00:37:20.279
Another area we seem to do things differently from others is

00:37:20.299 --> 00:37:23.759
management succession in general, and CEO succession in

00:37:23.779 --> 00:37:25.049
particular.

00:37:25.069 --> 00:37:28.159
Years ago at Norwest, we put in the policy that all members

00:37:28.179 --> 00:37:31.119
of the management committee, the top fifty or so people in

00:37:31.139 --> 00:37:37.110
our company, had to retire not later than the end of the

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:38.639
year that they became 65.

00:37:38.659 --> 00:37:43.190
Now, this was not done because we think people over 65

00:37:43.210 --> 00:37:45.920
cannot continue to contribute to corporations.

00:37:45.940 --> 00:37:49.059
It was done for succession-planning purposes.

00:37:49.079 --> 00:37:52.019
It takes many years to prepare someone to take over senior

00:37:52.039 --> 00:37:54.569
positions at a company the size and complexity of Wells

00:37:54.589 --> 00:37:55.589
Fargo.

00:37:55.589 --> 00:37:58.529
For senior positions, it may take three to five years; for

00:37:58.549 --> 00:38:00.639
CEO position, even longer.

00:38:00.659 --> 00:38:04.089
The ?65 and you?re out? policy was put in place to ensure

00:38:04.109 --> 00:38:06.609
that everyone, including the board, knew there was a time

00:38:06.509 --> 00:38:09.420
certain that someone would retire, and so plans could be

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:11.940
made to select the best candidates and give them the

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:14.529
necessary experience to handle the new job.

00:38:14.549 --> 00:38:17.799
So, we ask all committee members to give us a minimum of two

00:38:17.159 --> 00:38:19.349
years? notice.

00:38:19.369 --> 00:38:23.239
And so, the ?65 and you?re out? policy is really something

00:38:23.259 --> 00:38:24.759
more akin to a term limit.

00:38:24.759 --> 00:38:28.049
It ensures there will be a well-timed process to select the

00:38:28.069 --> 00:38:30.889
best placement, and the replacement candidate would also

00:38:30.909 --> 00:38:33.329
know approximately when the position would be available.

00:38:33.349 --> 00:38:36.349
It could be sooner, but it would not be later than that

00:38:35.859 --> 00:38:36.859
date.

00:38:36.859 --> 00:38:41.549
And that's how we handled CEO succession at Wells Fargo.

00:38:41.569 --> 00:38:45.460
I don't think the old CEO should stay beyond 65 to play the

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:51.329
role of coach, as the new CEO is selected about 18 months to

00:38:51.349 --> 00:38:56.289
two years before becoming CEO, and the old CEO becomes

00:38:56.309 --> 00:38:59.360
chairman and helps coach the succession process.

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:02.880
I think the old CEO should give up his CEO role in that two

00:39:02.799 --> 00:39:07.009
year period and remain a full-time chairman until his age of

00:39:07.029 --> 00:39:08.949
65 expires.

00:39:08.969 --> 00:39:12.219
Now, Wells Fargo followed this process with our CEO

00:39:12.239 --> 00:39:15.179
succession, and in fact, that CEO succession started about

00:39:15.199 --> 00:39:21.860
seven years before John Stump was selected in July of 2007.

00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:26.329
By the way, I would have retired at the end of last year,

00:39:26.349 --> 00:39:29.119
but because we acquired Wachovia, and because of the

00:39:29.139 --> 00:39:32.699
financial crisis, I was asked to stay on for an extra year

00:39:32.719 --> 00:39:37.559
to help integrate the acquisition and help with the crisis.

00:39:37.579 --> 00:39:44.299
Let me conclude by asking you this question.

00:39:44.319 --> 00:39:47.699
What is the most important metric in business?

00:39:47.719 --> 00:39:51.420
If you had to pick only one metric to evaluate how well a

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:55.099
company is performing, and how likely it would perform in

00:39:55.119 --> 00:40:00.619
the future, what would that be?

00:40:00.639 --> 00:40:03.639
If I have learned anything over the past 40 years, it is

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:08.210
this: the key to the bottom line is actually the top line.

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:11.619
The only way to consistently grow profits is to consistently

00:40:11.639 --> 00:40:14.719
grow organic risk-adjusted revenue.

00:40:14.739 --> 00:40:17.239
If customers are giving you more of their business, as

00:40:17.039 --> 00:40:20.009
evidenced by organic, revenue growth, you know you must be

00:40:20.029 --> 00:40:22.250
doing a lot of things right.

00:40:22.269 --> 00:40:24.949
You must have competitive products, you must have good

00:40:24.969 --> 00:40:27.869
service, you must have good people who understand customer's

00:40:27.889 --> 00:40:31.319
needs, you must be priced about right, your technology must

00:40:31.339 --> 00:40:32.569
be competitive.

00:40:32.589 --> 00:40:34.500
The opposite is also true.

00:40:34.519 --> 00:40:37.679
If you?re not generating organic revenue, you have major

00:40:37.699 --> 00:40:40.389
problems.

00:40:40.409 --> 00:40:43.960
Cutting costs only lasts for so long, and will probably even

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:45.170
reduce future revenue.

00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:49.599
Financial engineering is more often a long-term negative

00:40:49.619 --> 00:40:51.529
than a positive.

00:40:51.549 --> 00:40:54.589
If you believe that revenue is the key metric then, as CEO,

00:40:54.609 --> 00:40:56.449
you must believe by example.

00:40:56.469 --> 00:40:59.759
You must be customer-focused, call on customers, make sales?

00:40:59.779 --> 00:41:02.119
calls with team members, and walk the talk.

00:41:02.139 --> 00:41:05.239
I have probably made an average of two customer calls or

00:41:05.259 --> 00:41:08.779
customer visits every day in my business career.

00:41:08.799 --> 00:41:12.119
I don't ever remember that being considered important when I

00:41:12.139 --> 00:41:14.589
was at the Stanford Business School.

00:41:14.609 --> 00:41:17.650
Acquisitions that grow revenue after being acquired can

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:20.190
never be good investments for very long.

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:23.519
In my opinion, revenue growth is so important that you

00:41:23.539 --> 00:41:26.739
shouldn't even make an acquisition unless the revenue growth

00:41:26.759 --> 00:41:29.889
of the combined enterprise is higher than the sum of the

00:41:29.909 --> 00:41:32.869
revenue growth of the two companies if they had remained

00:41:32.889 --> 00:41:33.889
separate entities.

00:41:33.889 --> 00:41:38.630
So, in my opinion, it's all about organic risk-adjusted

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:43.699
revenue growth year after year after year.

00:41:43.719 --> 00:41:47.029
By the way, Wells Fargo has been growing revenue at double-

00:41:47.049 --> 00:41:50.389
digit rates for 20 years, 12 percent annual compound rate,

00:41:50.409 --> 00:41:52.389
for example.

00:41:52.409 --> 00:41:55.500
So, it's indeed been quite a story and quite a journey.

00:41:55.519 --> 00:41:58.480
It is really a story of the opportunity presented by

00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:00.559
capitalism and the free enterprise system.

00:42:00.579 --> 00:42:03.809
It's amazing what opportunities are out there.

00:42:03.829 --> 00:42:06.579
Speaking of capitalism, I thought you'd like to know my

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:09.079
definition of capitalism and how it differs from other

00:42:09.099 --> 00:42:10.739
political and economic systems.

00:42:10.759 --> 00:42:14.179
It is particularly relevant, given what's going on in

00:42:14.199 --> 00:42:15.199
Washington today.

00:42:15.199 --> 00:42:17.339
First, Socialism.

00:42:17.359 --> 00:42:20.589
That's when you have two cows, and the government gives one

00:42:20.609 --> 00:42:22.779
to your neighbor.

00:42:22.799 --> 00:42:23.799
Communism.

00:42:23.519 --> 00:42:27.019
That's when you have two cows, you had to give both to the

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:28.960
government, and the government distributes half the milk to

00:42:28.960 --> 00:42:31.960
the people and the other half spoils on the way to being

00:42:31.210 --> 00:42:32.210
delivered

00:42:32.210 --> 00:42:33.829
Fascism.

00:42:33.849 --> 00:42:35.599
That's when you have two cows.

00:42:35.279 --> 00:42:38.029
You have to give all the milk to the government officials.

00:42:37.909 --> 00:42:40.779
The officials sell the milk and deposit the proceeds in

00:42:40.799 --> 00:42:43.279
their Swiss bank account.

00:42:43.299 --> 00:42:45.219
And then there's capitalism.

00:42:45.239 --> 00:42:47.079
You have two cows.

00:42:47.099 --> 00:42:49.730
You sell one of them, and you buy a bull.

00:42:49.750 --> 00:42:50.730
&gt;&gt;

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:51.750
[laughter]

00:42:51.589 --> 00:42:52.650
That's capitalism.

00:42:52.670 --> 00:43:01.339
If I have learned anything in my 42 years of business, it is

00:43:01.359 --> 00:43:05.909
this: Success, without fun, never lasts.

00:43:05.929 --> 00:43:10.679
Fun without success isn't much fun.

00:43:10.699 --> 00:43:14.199
My wish for all of you is to have both fun and success in

00:43:14.199 --> 00:43:15.199
your business career.

00:43:15.199 --> 00:43:18.130
Thank you for inviting me back to my roots.

00:43:18.150 --> 00:43:21.650
I wish all of you the best in your career, and you have as

00:43:20.710 --> 00:43:22.719
much fun as I?ve had in mine.

00:43:22.739 --> 00:43:25.739
I'd now be happy to answer any questions you may have.

00:43:25.489 --> 00:43:26.489
Thank you very much.

00:43:26.489 --> 00:43:26.689
&gt;&gt;

00:43:26.489 --> 00:43:27.489
[applause]

00:43:26.489 --> 00:43:39.289
Q: Thank you for your talk.

00:43:39.309 --> 00:43:42.309
I read a quote from Alan Greenspan nine months or so ago,

00:43:42.119 --> 00:43:45.369
where he said if you were 50 years younger, he?d start a

00:43:44.659 --> 00:43:45.659
bank.

00:43:45.659 --> 00:43:48.659
I?m wondering if you agree with that sentiment, and if you

00:43:48.659 --> 00:43:51.659
do, how that might apply to someone in our shoes that?s

00:43:51.659 --> 00:43:53.159
going out into the working world.

00:43:53.159 --> 00:43:56.659
A Well, I don't know about starting a bank, because it's a

00:43:56.159 --> 00:43:59.159
difficult thing to do and takes time and a lot of capital

00:43:59.039 --> 00:44:02.650
too, but I think it's financial services business ? might as

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:05.670
well change the word from bank branch ? is one of the great

00:44:05.420 --> 00:44:06.859
industries in the world.

00:44:06.879 --> 00:44:09.609
You know, money never declines.

00:44:09.629 --> 00:44:16.559
It grows forever, and you're basically in the money business

00:44:16.579 --> 00:44:18.079
if you?re in financial services.

00:44:18.079 --> 00:44:21.440
And so, you have an industry that's always going up and

00:44:21.460 --> 00:44:23.389
there's not many of that.

00:44:23.409 --> 00:44:26.659
You know, you can have too much food, you can have too much

00:44:26.549 --> 00:44:29.799
wine, but I don't know of anyone who has too much money.

00:44:29.349 --> 00:44:29.549
&gt;&gt;

00:44:29.549 --> 00:44:30.549
[laughter]

00:44:29.549 --> 00:44:31.549
So, I think it's a great business.

00:44:31.230 --> 00:44:34.179
Q You mentioned your business units were all very

00:44:34.199 --> 00:44:35.199
decentralized.

00:44:35.199 --> 00:44:37.699
How did you leverage, I guess, the integrated side of

00:44:37.639 --> 00:44:38.639
things?

00:44:38.639 --> 00:44:41.819
How did you manage your business units so they?re integrated

00:44:41.839 --> 00:44:42.839
while being also decentralized?

00:44:42.839 --> 00:44:43.839
A Yeah.

00:44:43.339 --> 00:44:44.839
It's really our secret sauce.

00:44:44.670 --> 00:44:48.489
We operate these decentralized businesses, but from a

00:44:48.509 --> 00:44:51.339
customer standpoint, we hope to be viewed as one Wells

00:44:51.359 --> 00:44:52.359
Fargo.

00:44:52.359 --> 00:44:57.369
So, we partner between the businesses so that we can deliver

00:44:57.389 --> 00:45:00.239
all the various products and services, that these

00:45:00.259 --> 00:45:04.279
decentralized companies are manufacturing, as one, to the

00:45:04.299 --> 00:45:05.299
customer.

00:45:05.299 --> 00:45:07.880
It's a very difficult process.

00:45:07.900 --> 00:45:11.150
It's kind of all explained in our vision and values but it

00:45:10.750 --> 00:45:15.319
really ? you have to completely organize your company from

00:45:15.339 --> 00:45:20.069
your technology to your incentive systems to your structure

00:45:20.089 --> 00:45:25.059
to your reward and recognition system that encourages this

00:45:25.079 --> 00:45:26.079
behavior.

00:45:26.079 --> 00:45:30.029
And it's literally hundreds of small things that make that

00:45:30.049 --> 00:45:31.109
happen.

00:45:31.129 --> 00:45:36.329
And the way I explain this is, the bad news is, This is hard

00:45:36.349 --> 00:45:38.029
to do.

00:45:38.049 --> 00:45:42.029
The good news is, This is hard to do.

00:45:42.049 --> 00:45:44.299
And it's a little bit like Wal-Mart.

00:45:44.009 --> 00:45:48.569
Does anyone not know what Wal-Mart's strategy is?

00:45:48.589 --> 00:45:52.289
Everyone knows it.

00:45:52.309 --> 00:45:53.509
Low cost etc.

00:45:53.529 --> 00:45:57.319
But it's how they do that strategy that nobody can

00:45:57.339 --> 00:45:58.679
duplicate.

00:45:58.699 --> 00:46:00.449
And it's a bit like ours.

00:46:00.199 --> 00:46:03.230
Everyone sees the benefit of cross-selling now, but it's

00:46:03.250 --> 00:46:06.250
very hard to do, so your competitive advantage is it's a

00:46:06.009 --> 00:46:08.569
superior business model.

00:46:08.589 --> 00:46:11.509
A superior business model that's easy doesn't stay superior

00:46:11.529 --> 00:46:12.529
for long.

00:46:12.529 --> 00:46:15.989
A bad business model that's hard is, of course, the worst

00:46:16.009 --> 00:46:17.009
thing you can do.

00:46:17.009 --> 00:46:17.489
&gt;&gt;

00:46:17.509 --> 00:46:18.509
[laughter]

00:46:17.509 --> 00:46:20.619
But a superior business model that's hard to do actually is

00:46:20.639 --> 00:46:23.659
the upper right hand corner.

00:46:23.679 --> 00:46:26.929
You can have a competitive advantage for a long time if it's

00:46:26.569 --> 00:46:28.069
a superior business model and hard.

00:46:28.069 --> 00:46:32.029
Q Just had a quick question.

00:46:32.049 --> 00:46:34.549
You know, you said you had already bid for Wachovia.

00:46:34.549 --> 00:46:38.000
There was a lot of coverage in the press, but what was the

00:46:38.019 --> 00:46:39.019
process?

00:46:39.019 --> 00:46:41.139
&gt;&gt; What process?

00:46:41.159 --> 00:46:45.449
&gt;&gt; Like bidding for Wachovia when Citi had already done so.

00:46:45.469 --> 00:46:49.929
And when the stock hit $7.80, or around that figure, did you

00:46:49.949 --> 00:46:54.309
ever think that it was a bad move back in March?

00:46:54.329 --> 00:46:57.599
&gt;&gt; Well, no, I guess, is the short answer.

00:46:57.619 --> 00:47:02.460
You know it gets back to what I was trying to say before.

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:08.309
You know, the convention wisdom ? in my experience, if you

00:47:08.329 --> 00:47:13.130
only had two choices to make, follow the conventional wisdom

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:15.150
or do the opposite.

00:47:15.170 --> 00:47:17.670
If you did the opposite you would be better off.

00:47:17.059 --> 00:47:20.309
I'm not saying you should do that, but I'm saying the

00:47:19.809 --> 00:47:23.849
conventional wisdom is wrong, in my opinion, so much of the

00:47:23.869 --> 00:47:25.289
time.

00:47:25.309 --> 00:47:28.309
But again, you got to pay attention to it, because there's

00:47:28.210 --> 00:47:29.779
usually some truth to it.

00:47:29.799 --> 00:47:32.679
But you just have to have your own conviction.

00:47:32.699 --> 00:47:33.949
Come to your own conclusion.

00:47:33.949 --> 00:47:35.259
Do that quantitative analysis.

00:47:35.279 --> 00:47:38.559
Listen to your people.

00:47:38.579 --> 00:47:39.769
Forget ego.

00:47:39.789 --> 00:47:46.779
Do the right thing, and usually, it will be opposite or

00:47:46.799 --> 00:47:49.519
different from the conventional wisdom, and that's how you

00:47:49.539 --> 00:47:50.539
do things.

00:47:50.539 --> 00:47:53.750
Again, when do you buy?

00:47:53.769 --> 00:47:58.299
You buy when things are great or when things are in trouble?

00:47:58.319 --> 00:47:59.569
And when do people buy?

00:47:59.269 --> 00:48:00.789
And this is not hard.

00:48:00.809 --> 00:48:04.980
If you have a cyclical business, if you just buy it here and

00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:07.750
perform on average, you're going to perform a lot better

00:48:07.650 --> 00:48:09.400
than if you buy it up here.

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:09.900
It's cyclical.

00:48:09.900 --> 00:48:11.900
Almost all businesses are cyclical.

00:48:11.920 --> 00:48:13.420
So it's things like that.

00:48:13.029 --> 00:48:19.900
So our market share and our acquisitions strategy has always

00:48:19.920 --> 00:48:24.519
been greater by huge degree in the so-called bad times.

00:48:24.539 --> 00:48:30.239
Q So you talk a lot about acquisitions and culture.

00:48:30.259 --> 00:48:32.509
How would you characterize the difference between Wachovia's

00:48:32.449 --> 00:48:35.449
culture and Wells Fargo's and what have you done to really

00:48:35.349 --> 00:48:38.099
sort of done blend the two cultures together to have a

00:48:37.659 --> 00:48:38.659
cohesive unit?

00:48:38.659 --> 00:48:45.849
A The problem that happened with Wachovia is, they started

00:48:45.869 --> 00:48:54.049
out as a regional bank and made a lot of acquisitions where

00:48:54.069 --> 00:48:58.279
the theory was ? and the former CEO, ______, basically said,

00:48:58.299 --> 00:49:01.179
?I?ve got to get bigger to be better.?

00:49:01.199 --> 00:49:04.449
And that's, as I said earlier in my speech, that's very

00:49:03.349 --> 00:49:04.349
dangerous.

00:49:04.349 --> 00:49:07.099
And he bought things at very high prices, and got into

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:07.900
problems.

00:49:07.900 --> 00:49:09.859
So then, they had to cut costs.

00:49:09.879 --> 00:49:13.889
Then, he decided to go into brand new businesses because

00:49:13.909 --> 00:49:17.400
they were fairly narrowly focused, saw the benefits or

00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:20.210
diversity a little late, and thought they had to catch up.

00:49:20.230 --> 00:49:24.409
And so, they went into investment banking.

00:49:24.429 --> 00:49:27.179
They went into the mortgage business at the peak of the

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:28.000
cycle.

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:32.619
And so, what happened is, they had a lot of problems that

00:49:32.639 --> 00:49:39.289
were caused by the failed implementation of a strategy, but

00:49:39.309 --> 00:49:42.250
the fundamental businesses were actually quite strong.

00:49:42.269 --> 00:49:45.690
They?re being masked by the mistakes they made, by going

00:49:45.710 --> 00:49:48.210
into the investment banking and trading, by going into the

00:49:48.219 --> 00:49:50.429
pick-a-pay in the savings business.

00:49:50.449 --> 00:49:54.219
They way-overpaid for certain acquisitions, and had to make

00:49:54.239 --> 00:49:56.250
that up by cost cutting and so on.

00:49:56.269 --> 00:50:00.639
And we know what happened, and all we've done is sliced off

00:50:00.659 --> 00:50:03.909
all the bad things ? we don't do the things that got them

00:50:03.449 --> 00:50:04.449
into trouble.

00:50:04.449 --> 00:50:09.130
We are paying part of the purchase price of the difference

00:50:09.150 --> 00:50:10.920
between 90 billion and 12.7

00:50:10.940 --> 00:50:13.940
is writing off all of the problems in their pick-a-pay

00:50:13.699 --> 00:50:17.019
portfolio, but the underlying people are very culturally

00:50:17.039 --> 00:50:22.719
similar, the businesses are very similar, and this will turn

00:50:22.739 --> 00:50:25.239
out to be the best deal ever done in banking.

00:50:24.579 --> 00:50:29.940
Q Thank you so much for joining us today.

00:50:29.960 --> 00:50:34.150
My question is, you mentioned earlier that you have a very

00:50:34.170 --> 00:50:37.069
keen focus on revenue growth and you also mentioned that you

00:50:37.089 --> 00:50:39.589
guys are expanding your other business lines, such as

00:50:39.609 --> 00:50:41.929
investment banking, but actually those two things in

00:50:41.949 --> 00:50:44.469
combination, many people said over the last five years, are

00:50:44.489 --> 00:50:47.279
precisely what led us into the crisis today, because retail

00:50:47.299 --> 00:50:50.069
banks were focusing on growing the revenue, diversifying all

00:50:50.089 --> 00:50:53.379
their risk on Wall Street, and that's kind of how we ended

00:50:53.399 --> 00:50:55.399
up in the situation we had last year.

00:50:55.399 --> 00:50:58.649
So, how do you feel as a bank, you guys can move forward

00:50:58.649 --> 00:51:01.649
with those two things in mind and not face whatever the next

00:51:01.399 --> 00:51:02.399
crisis may be?

00:51:02.399 --> 00:51:05.650
A Well, as I said, I think the cultures that existed back

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:08.869
then we wouldn?t have gone into investment banking.

00:51:08.889 --> 00:51:09.889
That has changed.

00:51:09.889 --> 00:51:12.639
Now, when I talk about investment banking, I'm talking about

00:51:12.389 --> 00:51:14.389
the meat and potatoes of investment banking, the

00:51:14.389 --> 00:51:16.739
underwriting, debt and equity, and so on.

00:51:16.759 --> 00:51:20.839
We did not do, and will not do the trading activities, the

00:51:20.859 --> 00:51:23.529
CDOs, the structured products.

00:51:23.549 --> 00:51:26.969
They didn't make any sense anyway.

00:51:26.989 --> 00:51:31.480
So, but we couldn't even be in the basic part of the

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:34.279
investment banking, because of the cultures that exist, but

00:51:34.299 --> 00:51:36.000
it's all gone now.

00:51:36.019 --> 00:51:40.839
So, today, we think as I said, we were probably in the top

00:51:40.859 --> 00:51:43.109
ten; now, we'll be in the top five.

00:51:42.849 --> 00:51:46.799
Now, if that culture changes back to the old way, we will

00:51:46.819 --> 00:51:49.319
just wind that business down if we can't compete.

00:51:49.329 --> 00:51:50.829
I don't see that happening.

00:51:50.359 --> 00:51:53.609
I think this is a fundamental change that's going to be

00:51:53.629 --> 00:51:58.259
around, if not forever, for at least ten years.

00:51:58.279 --> 00:52:03.730
And, if anything, I think the industry will move even closer

00:52:03.750 --> 00:52:10.230
to a culture like Wells Fargo than it even is today, and one

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:14.019
reason that will occur is I think you're going to have close

00:52:14.039 --> 00:52:17.650
watch by regulators in this industry for a long time.

00:52:17.670 --> 00:52:21.000
Even if they wanted to, they're not going to be allowed to

00:52:21.019 --> 00:52:24.519
do it, other than going into an unregulated hedge fund or

00:52:24.539 --> 00:52:27.289
whatever, but it's not going to be done through investment

00:52:26.980 --> 00:52:31.150
banking, in my opinion. We'll see.

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:40.269
Q I worked at Perella Weinberg, and was an adviser in the

00:52:40.289 --> 00:52:44.829
Wachovia-Wells Fargo transaction, and you guys really have

00:52:44.849 --> 00:52:46.849
something unique.

00:52:46.869 --> 00:52:49.879
And you spoke about choosing people was your key role

00:52:49.899 --> 00:52:53.049
I was just wondering if you could offer some advice as far

00:52:53.069 --> 00:52:55.869
as, when you?re choosing people, what were the key

00:52:55.889 --> 00:52:58.389
components or any advice related to that?

00:52:58.409 --> 00:53:03.789
A Well, as I say, most of our senior people are home-grown.

00:53:03.809 --> 00:53:08.210
We like to bring people in early in life, experience the

00:53:08.230 --> 00:53:12.109
culture, see the most successful ones, and promote them,

00:53:12.129 --> 00:53:13.129
etc.

00:53:13.129 --> 00:53:20.559
Mostly, outside people we bring in are through acquisitions.

00:53:20.579 --> 00:53:24.920
So again, we don't bring a lot of people in that get into

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:27.940
senior positions that haven't been with us for at least ten

00:53:27.429 --> 00:53:28.429
years.

00:53:28.429 --> 00:53:31.429
So it's kind of home grown is how we do that.

00:53:31.179 --> 00:53:36.039
And we want people who have great values, who are smart but

00:53:36.059 --> 00:53:40.639
do believe in teamwork, and really caring about customers

00:53:40.659 --> 00:53:42.159
and their communities and so forth.

00:53:42.069 --> 00:53:45.109
And we think that's more important, as I said, than brains

00:53:45.129 --> 00:53:47.728
at the 99 percent level.

00:53:47.748 --> 00:53:47.948
&gt;&gt;

00:53:46.748 --> 00:53:47.748
[clapping]

